#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# x_scale

#% text
# `x_scale` is an option of every plot: `x_scale: linear`, `log`, `signed_log` or `signed_log(c)` sets the scale of the x axis.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `signed_log(c)` maps `v` to `sign(v)*log10(1 + abs(v)/c)` (default `c = 1`): linear near 0 and logarithmic far from it, for both signs.

#% item
# A `log` scale needs positive values.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
plot(log(x), [x, 0.01, 100], x_scale: log)
#% output
#= image("x_scale.files/x_scale-1.svgz")

#% cell
plot(x, [x, -100, 100], x_scale: signed_log)
#% output
#= image("x_scale.files/x_scale-2.svgz")

#% cell
list_plot([[1, 1], [10, 2], [100, 3], [1000, 4]], x_scale: log)
#% output
#= image("x_scale.files/x_scale-3.svgz")

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Both axes logarithmic, a power law as a straight line:

#% cell
plot(x^-2, [x, 1, 100], x_scale: log, y_scale: log)
#% output
#= image("x_scale.files/x_scale-4.svgz")

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `y_scale`, `log_log_plot`, `log_plot`
